<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
   RT-LOOP SIGNAL FILTERS  |  config/filters.xml
  ============================================================================

  Assigns a filter to each derived joint/gripper signal. HOT-RELOADABLE: edit
  this file, click "Reload Filters" in the GUI (RT Diagnostics tab), and RTI
  rebuilds the filters on its next tick. No restart, no re-home. Each filter's
  internal state resets on reload, so expect a brief transient right after.

  If this file is missing or invalid, RTI logs a warning and falls back to the
  built-in defaults, so a typo here can never stop the robot from booting.


  ============================================================================
   1. SIGNALS  (the elements allowed inside <filters>)
  ============================================================================
  Each element is a SIGNAL, not a joint. Its filter applies to every joint of
  that signal (see section 4 for treating one joint differently).

    <speed>          filters joint_speed          into joint_speed_filtered
    <accel>          filters joint_acceleration   into joint_acceleration_filtered
    <jerk>           filters joint_jerk           into joint_jerk_filtered
    <torque>         filters joint_torque         into joint_torque_filtered
    <gripper_speed>  filters gripper_joint_speed  into gripper_joint_speed_filtered

  The no-suffix signals (joint_speed, joint_acceleration, ...) stay RAW and
  unfiltered; only the _filtered twins are produced here. TCP velocity /
  acceleration and torque_external are computed from the FILTERED signals.

  Any signal you omit uses its built-in default (shown in the active block at
  the bottom). gripper_speed is a single channel, so per-joint overrides do not
  apply to it.


  ============================================================================
   2. FILTER TYPES  (the "type" attribute)
  ============================================================================
    none      needs nothing.   Passthrough. Also the honest choice when a
                               signal is already clean enough.
    ema       needs tau.       1-pole low-pass. Cheapest. The old default.
    ema2      needs tau.       2-pole (two ema stages). The jerk fix.
    dema      needs tau.       Lag-REDUCED ema. NOT a 2-pole low-pass, will
                               NOT clean jerk. Use when lag hurts, not noise.
    sma       needs window.    Moving average. Linear (constant) phase.
    butter2   needs tau.       2-pole Butterworth. Flatter passband than ema2
                               for the same lag; cutoff = 1/(2*pi*tau) Hz.
    savgol    needs window     Fits a polynomial over the window. Purpose-built
              plus poly.       for noisy derivatives; linear phase.
    median    needs window.    The ONLY non-linear one: removes a lone encoder
                               spike instead of smearing it. Great vs glitches.


  ============================================================================
   3. PARAMETER NOMENCLATURE  (the attributes)
  ============================================================================
    type="ema2"    Which filter (section 2). Required on every signal / joint.

    tau="0.03"     TIME CONSTANT in SECONDS. Bigger tau = smoother = more lag.
                   Used by ema, ema2, dema, butter2. The window kinds (sma,
                   savgol, median) also accept tau and derive a window from it.
                   tau is delta_t independent: the same tau is the same real
                   filter at 250 Hz and at 100 Hz (RTI converts it per robot).

    window="7"     Number of samples. Must be an ODD integer, 3 or greater.
                   Used by sma, savgol, median. Bigger window = smoother = more
                   lag. Roughly, window is about 2*tau/delta_t.

    poly="2"       Polynomial order for savgol ONLY. Must be less than window.
                   Higher poly tracks curvature better but smooths less.

    idx="4"        Joint index (0 to joint_num minus 1) for a per-joint override.
                   Numbering matches the CAN nodes: 0 = base ... 5 = wrist 3.


  ============================================================================
   4. PER-JOINT OVERRIDES  (tutorial)
  ============================================================================
  A signal's attributes are the DEFAULT for all its joints. To treat one joint
  differently, nest a <joint idx="N"> inside the signal. The override INHERITS
  the signal's settings and REPLACES ONLY the attributes it names. All 8 types
  are allowed on a per-joint basis.

  Example A. Same filter type on every joint, one joint smoothed harder:

      <jerk type="ema2" tau="0.03">
          <joint idx="5" tau="0.06"/>
      </jerk>

      Result: J0 to J4 = ema2 tau=0.03  (the default)
              J5        = ema2 tau=0.06  (inherited ema2, only tau changed)

  Example B. A different filter TYPE on one joint (say a flaky encoder that
  throws single-sample spikes needs median, the rest are fine on ema2):

      <jerk type="ema2" tau="0.03">
          <joint idx="4" type="median" window="5"/>
      </jerk>

      Result: J0..J3, J5 = ema2 tau=0.03
              J4          = median window=5

  Example C. Several joints, each independent:

      <accel type="none">
          <joint idx="0" type="ema"     tau="0.02"/>
          <joint idx="3" type="butter2" tau="0.03"/>
          <joint idx="4" type="savgol"  window="9" poly="2"/>
      </accel>

      Result: J1, J2, J5 = none        (the default: passthrough)
              J0          = ema tau=0.02
              J3          = butter2 tau=0.03
              J4          = savgol window=9 poly=2

  WHEN TO USE PER-JOINT: for a single joint with a genuinely PERSISTENT hardware
  problem (a bad encoder or cable). NOT for routine tuning. Measured on this arm,
  the at-rest noise is encoder LSB dithering, and WHICH joint is noisy reshuffles
  between power cycles, so a per-joint tune done today can land on the wrong joint
  after the next boot. The stable default is one uniform tau per signal, sized
  for the worst-case joint. You will rarely need <joint> at all.


  ============================================================================
   5. THE RULE THAT MATTERS  (choosing type per signal)
  ============================================================================
  A signal needs as many filter POLES as it has DIFFERENTIATIONS. Each derivative
  amplifies high-frequency noise; each 1-pole filter stage removes one octave of
  that amplification. No amount of tau tuning substitutes for a missing pole.

    speed, torque   measured directly (0 differentiations): 1 pole (ema) or none
    accel           1 differentiation: 1 pole. Its input is already the FILTERED
                    speed, which supplies that pole, so the default is "none"
                    (a 2nd filter here measured WORSE, not better).
    jerk            2 differentiations: 2 poles (ema2 / butter2 / savgol). A
                    single ema CANNOT clean jerk at ANY tau.

  TUNING WORKFLOW: the tau values below are starting points from a rest-noise
  measurement. To refine, jog the arm, watch the live filtered signal in the GUI,
  edit tau here, click Reload Filters, repeat. No re-home between iterations.
  ============================================================================
-->
<filters>
    <speed type="butter2" tau="0.0101"/>   <!-- measured (0 diffs): 1 pole is plenty -->
    <accel type="dema" tau="0.0051"/>   <!-- input already carries speed's pole; "none" measured BEST -->
    <jerk type="ema" tau="0.1351"/>   <!-- 2 diffs: needs 2 POLES (ema2/butter2/savgol) - 1-pole ema cannot fix it at any tau -->
    <torque type="ema" tau="0.1001"/>   <!-- measured (0 diffs): 1 pole. Also cleans torque_external/force_external -->
    <tcp_accel type="ema" tau="0.0246"/>   <!-- Cartesian accel; its Jdot*qdot term is quadratic in velocity -->
    <gripper_speed type="ema" tau="0.02"/>   <!-- measured (0 diffs): 1 pole -->
</filters>
